    <h2>Storage: the most common money leak in a move</h2>
    <p>Storage units are easy to rent and easy to forget. Rent is usually low at the start, and many facilities raise rates on existing tenants after the first months. The <a href="../../calculators/storage-unit-keep-or-sell.html">storage unit calculator</a> finds the month when total rent passes what it would cost to sell your things now and buy replacements later. In its default example, a $165-a-month unit costs about $825 more over 2 years than selling and rebuying.</p>
    <p>Sort before you store. Items that are hard to replace, such as family heirlooms, documents, and expensive furniture you'll definitely use again, are worth storing. Mass-market furniture, appliances, and things you haven't missed in a year usually aren't. If you do rent, choose the smallest unit that fits, ask about rate increases in writing, and set a date to decide again.</p>

    <h2>Moving: truck, movers, or container</h2>
    <p>The <a href="../../calculators/moving-cost.html">moving cost calculator</a> compares the 3 main options with costs that quotes leave out: fuel, paid helpers, lodging on long moves, and your own time.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rental truck:</strong> usually cheapest in cash, but you do the loading, driving, and unloading. Truck rental is priced by days and miles, and big trucks get poor fuel economy.</li>
      <li><strong>Portable container:</strong> you pack at your own pace and the company drives. Good when your new place isn't ready yet, since many companies can store the container in between.</li>
      <li><strong>Full-service movers:</strong> the most expensive, but the least work and risk to your back. Prices for long moves depend heavily on weight and season.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Getting moving quotes you can trust</h2>
    <p>Get at least 3 written estimates based on an in-home or video survey of your belongings, not a phone guess. For interstate moves in the U.S., check that the mover is registered with the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration and understand the difference between a binding estimate, which fixes the price, and a non-binding one, which can change with the actual weight. Movers' basic liability coverage pays far less than most items are worth; consider buying additional valuation coverage for anything valuable.</p>
  </div>
